---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import logo from "../assets/brand/logo.png";
---

<Base title="ai disclosure · ramus" description="How AI tools were used in making ramus.">
  <header class="wrap policy-top">
    <a class="policy-top__brand" href="/" aria-label="ramus, home">
      <Image src={logo} alt="" width={64} height={64} class="policy-top__mark" loading="eager" />
      <span>ramus</span>
    </a>
  </header>

  <main id="main" class="section policy-page">
    <article class="wrap policy">
      <h1 class="headline">ai disclosure.</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        <strong>tl;dr</strong> It was used (but not exclusively) to make a solodev hobby project take months, not years.
        all aspects of the design and how i wanted the app to be was on me. for better or worse.
      </p>

      <p>
        ramus (or the dream of something like it, a genre tree type music player) has been something that i have tried to
        build for well over a decade. I am a sysadmin by trade so not at all unfamiliar with some scripting, programming
        and systems hardening, but i am not a software developer. I am not going to pretend to be. If you are and you
        look at this code, you'll probably very quickly see anything i have written vs what claude has. I have tried to
        adhere to sensible and good programming principles and hopefully you can see from the history and how long i've
        been building this, it isn't just a one-shot quick slop thingy. I am using this all day every day and i want to
        make no money for it. its a labour of love.
      </p>

      <p>As for the history of this project, I have dabbled with trying to create this three times now in the past:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          It first started out as a very rough hacky foobar2000 plugin that i created for myself but ultimately gave up
          on because it was just really quite awful in practice.
        </li>
        <li>
          Then a few years ago, it was a verrrrry rough java proof of concept which worked, but I just didn't have the
          ability or time to get out of a "okay the genre tree works with my library and it can play music...now what"
          phase.
        </li>
        <li>
          About a year and a half ago, on and off with the tools and models available then, I started working on a swift
          based MacOS only version of ramus, and despite being pretty much feature ready and good to go, I ultimately
          decided to scrap it because over the course of building that, I had learned so much and I knew I could take
          that learning, and create something far better and multi platform (and also because swift is hella annoying
          to work with it turns out???)
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Skip forward some more and thanks to rapid recent advancements in genAI coding and that prior learning - have
        let me finally take this concept, and deliver it into something that I have been using every day now on
        multiple platforms for like nearly a year now. It has been feature complete and "ready to go" for a long time
        and all I have been doing is polish, security passes, and iterative improvements. This is open source and free,
        and I have no interest in ever making money for it, but I am proud of it, and I don't want to release something
        that sucks.
      </p>

      <p>
        ....ethically or philosophically, I think genAI is actually a very good fit for coding, and computers
        programming computers was probably always going to be the logical conclusion really.
      </p>

      <p>
        I however have absolutely zero interest in its existence or use in any other creativity etc (not that software
        development isnt creative but you know what I mean) so all design decisions here were me and if you can't tell
        by my godawful rambling writing style, so were the docs (mostly, save for the dry architectural stuff). And the
        logo. All 8 points on that path in illustrator and that one gradient oh yeah that year of graphic design at
        university is paying off big time.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* The privacy page's muted tint; this page has no sections for the
     scroll-driven tint to follow. */
  :root {
    --tint-a: #1c1b22;
    --tint-b: #23202b;
  }
</style>

<style>
  .policy-top {
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .policy-top__brand {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    font: 700 1.3rem/1 var(--font-display);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .policy-top__mark {
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
  }

  .policy-page {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .policy {
    max-width: calc(var(--measure) + 2 * var(--gutter));
    margin-inline: auto;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .policy :where(h1) {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .policy h2 {
    margin-top: 2.75rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .policy h2 + p,
  .policy h2 + ul {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
  }

  .policy ul {
    margin: 0.85rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  /* Unlike the other pages this one is running prose, so paragraphs need
     their own spacing. */
  .policy > p + p,
  .policy > ul + p {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
  }

  .policy > .lede + p {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .policy li + li {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .policy strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
